      <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">A declaration of trust creates a revocable living trust: you name yourself grantor and trustee, keep full control while living, and name successor trustees and beneficiaries who take over without probate. State law differences are smaller here than for land trusts — every state recognizes revocable living trusts — but statutory details like witnessing, notarization, and trust-registration requirements vary. Each state page covers what its law requires.</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top: 2rem;">What state pages cover</h2>
      <p>For your state you'll find: whether the declaration must be notarized or witnessed, any state-specific registration or filing requirement, how to retitle real estate into the trust, the state's rules on homestead and community property inside a trust, and how successor trustees take over. Where a state has a short-form summary statute (a certificate of trust), the page links to it — that's the document banks accept.</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top: 2rem;">After signing: fund the trust</h2>
      <p>The declaration alone avoids no probate. Transfer each asset: record new deeds for real estate, retitle accounts, and update beneficiary designations. The <a href="/guide/trust-funding-checklist/">trust funding checklist</a> walks through every asset type, and the <a href="/create/certificate-of-trust/">certificate of trust generator</a> produces the summary document institutions ask for.</p>
      <p>Create your declaration with the <a href="/create/declaration-of-trust/">free declaration of trust generator</a>.</p>
